{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00056 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin is associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a series of indications that link it to different subtypes of AML, including those with Npm1 somatic mutations and T(9;11)(p22;q23) translocations. The evidence suggests that Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin is used in the treatment of AML with Npm1 mutations, which are a common genetic alteration in AML. This indication is further supported by its association with Quizartinib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor, which is also linked to AML with T(9;11)(p22;q23) translocations. The presence of multiple pathways involving Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin and different AML subtypes indicates a potential role in the treatment of AML with T(9;11)(p22;q23) translocations, although the exact mechanism of action and clinical relevance remain to be fully elucidated. The scores from the visible paths suggest a strong relationship, but further research is needed to confirm the therapeutic utility and mechanisms of action in this specific subset of AML.",
